        <body>
            <div xml:id="d341">

                <pb facs="0676" n="552" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg552"/>

                <head>No. 337.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hoppin to Mr. Blaine</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-11-14"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 14, 1881</date>.
                        (Received November 29.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 220.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your telegram of the 2d of
                    May, and to your instruction No. 172 of the 2d of June last, in relation to the
                    case of Mr. Joseph B. Walsh, I have the honor to acquaint you that I have been
                    informed by Lord Granville that he has been discharged from Kilmainham prison on
                    account of the state of his health. Lord Granville has forwarded to me
                    confidentially the order to this effect, a copy of which <pb facs="0677" n="553"
                        xml:id="pg553"/> I have the honor to inclose, as well as copies of Lord
                    Granville’s letter and that of Mr. Lowell’s to which it was a reply.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>W. T. HOPPIN.</signed></closer>





